# Reload Lifecycle Coordinator Implementation Plan

Source proposal: `Docs/reload-lifecycle-coordinator-proposal.md`

## Goal

Replace service-specific recovery logic in `dwarfuicore/command.lua` with a
private, generation-neutral lifecycle coordinator. Generation-owned subsystems
must register exact quiescence, retirement, and discard callbacks; explicit
reload must recover tracked state deterministically without loading stale
module environments; ordinary API generation and health validation must remain
strict; partial reconstruction must be cleaned or retained under the proposal's
retry/restart rules; and process-stable tooltip render trampolines must retain
their exact adoptable identity.

This plan is planning-only. Every task remains unchecked until its stated
implementation and evidence are complete.

## Locked scope and non-goals

- [ ] Preserve `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')` and every public provider
  API; do not expose coordinator lifecycle operations to consumers.
- [ ] Preserve destructive Core-reload semantics for registrations, active
  context menus, UserPrompt work, tooltip intent, APIs, and handles.
- [ ] Do not make ordinary loading or service use tolerant of stale, malformed,
  or split-generation state.
- [ ] Do not automatically reload DwarfUI or introduce a general lifecycle
  framework for other plugins.
- [ ] Do not consolidate every process-owned slot into the service runtime.
- [ ] Do not implement pre-coordinator adoption, legacy-state inspection, or an
  upgrade-in-place adapter.
- [ ] Do not install packages or automatically restart Dwarf Fortress.
- [ ] Do not change production startup behavior or add a startup detector, gate,
  or automatic coordinator-activation path.
- [ ] Keep the first-activation restart operator-controlled and do not perform
  live coordinator activation before that restart is confirmed.

## Assumptions and resolved decisions

- [ ] Enforce the approved per-owner quiescence invariant: complete the ordered
  quiescence pass first, then retire every eligible owner whose own quiescence
  succeeded even if another owner failed.
- [ ] Treat `Docs/reload-lifecycle-coordinator-proposal.md` as authoritative for
  all other lifecycle, generation, failure, retry, restart, and validation
  behavior.
- [ ] Use `SERVICE_UNHEALTHY` for same-generation API or handle access to a
  disabled runtime; generation mismatch continues to take stale-error
  precedence (`STALE_API` or `STALE_HANDLE`).
- [ ] Require explicit operator confirmation that Dwarf Fortress was restarted
  before the first live activation of coordinator-bearing code.

## Phase 1: Characterize and freeze the current reload contract

Outcome: Existing successful and failed reload behavior is captured by focused
tests before coordinator production code changes.

- [ ] Inventory the current reload transaction and all process-owned slots in
  `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfuicore/command.lua`.
  - [ ] Map each tooltip, context-menu, UserPrompt, input-hook, registration,
    presenter, and service-runtime owner to its current construction, teardown,
    slot-discard, and successor behavior.
  - [ ] Record which callbacks can remain externally callable during teardown.
- [ ] Extend `tests/dwarfuicore_command.spec.lua` with characterization for:
  - [ ] successful reload and normal single-step generation advancement;
  - [ ] disabled-runtime retry;
  - [ ] split-generation tooltip recovery;
  - [ ] teardown failure and disabled-successor publication;
  - [ ] reconstruction failure and later corrected retry;
  - [ ] stale API and handle rejection after advancement; and
  - [ ] exact tooltip render-trampoline preservation and adoption.
- [ ] Add or extend focused owner tests in the existing tooltip, context-menu,
  service-provider, and UserPrompt suites for their current quiescence and
  retirement boundaries.
- [ ] Identify the existing live source-reload route that produces terminal
  status and cleanup evidence without modifying installed package files.
- [ ] Run the focused characterization suites before production changes and
  record their terminal results in the execution evidence ledger.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review mapping every characterization test to the
  proposal behavior it freezes before marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] The existing happy path, split-generation path, teardown failures,
  reconstruction failures, stale objects, and trampoline identity are covered
  by focused tests with recorded terminal results.
- [ ] Every initial lifecycle owner is mapped to an explicit proposed policy.
- [ ] The approved per-owner quiescence invariant is documented as an intended
  coordinator delta without requiring the pre-coordinator baseline to satisfy
  it.

## Phase 2: Add lifecycle contracts and process-stable coordinator state

Outcome: Private coordinator contracts, persistent state, validation, and
registration exist without yet replacing command orchestration.

- [ ] Add `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfuicore/reload_contracts.lua`.
  - [ ] Start the module with `--@ module=true` and load project modules through
    `reqscript()`.
  - [ ] Define immutable numeric enums for owner status and retirement order.
  - [ ] Include `ACTIVE`, `QUIESCING`, `QUIESCED`, `QUIESCE_FAILED`,
    `RETIRING`, `RETIRED`, and `RETIRE_FAILED`.
  - [ ] Assign deterministic retirement order for UserPrompt, context-menu,
    tooltip presentation, remaining input/registration owners, and discard-only
    owners.
- [ ] Add `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfuicore/reload_coordinator.lua`.
  - [ ] Start the module with `--@ module=true` and keep all operations private
    from `dwarfuicore/services`.
  - [ ] Adopt or create the process-stable coordinator state containing schema
    version, registration sequence, shared recovery guard, and records.
  - [ ] Validate schema and record structure without requiring coordinator state
    to match the service runtime generation.
  - [ ] Key records by runtime generation plus owner identifier so a partial
    successor cannot overwrite an older owner.
  - [ ] Implement structural registration validation for identifiers, positive
    matching generation, recognized order, callback function types, and
    uniqueness.
  - [ ] Return an opaque internal token for construction rollback and private
    diagnostics.
  - [ ] Reject malformed or untrusted records without invoking callbacks and
    report them as restart-requiring corruption.
  - [ ] Add LuaDoc for every new class and method.
- [ ] Add focused coordinator contract tests in
  `tests/reload_coordinator.spec.lua`.
  - [ ] Cover state creation/adoption, schema validation, uniqueness,
    generation validation, callback validation, sequence ordering, token
    opacity, and malformed-record diagnostics.
  - [ ] Prove registration failure can be paired with exact publication
    rollback and leaves no untracked process state.
- [ ] Register the new focused suite in `tests/run.lua`.
- [ ] Add `reload_contracts` and `reload_coordinator` to
  `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfuicore/module_registry.lua` before every owner
  module that can register lifecycle state.
  - [ ] Update `tests/module_registry.spec.lua` to prove the exact dependency
    order before coordinator-aware owner integration begins.
- [ ] Preserve service-provider identity and weak handle-identity stores as
  process-stable metadata across module-environment replacement.
  - [ ] Keep these stores outside coordinator generation-owned records and
    never make an old identity current again.
  - [ ] Add focused tests proving preserved identities still classify old APIs
    and handles as stale after generation advancement.
- [ ] Keep `service_provider_runtime` as command transaction authority and
  explicitly exclude it from coordinator owner registration.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of the contracts and tests against this phase
  before marking it complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Coordinator state survives module-environment replacement and accepts
  valid records from multiple runtime generations.
- [ ] Ordinary registration still requires the current service generation.
- [ ] Malformed records are never executed and are classified as restart
  blockers.
- [ ] Focused coordinator registration/state tests pass.

## Phase 3: Implement quiescence, retirement, discard, and retry semantics

Outcome: The coordinator can safely recover every trusted record through its
remaining lifecycle operations and produce complete aggregate diagnostics.

- [ ] Implement `recover_tracked_owners(runtime_generation)`.
  - [ ] Acquire the shared process-wide recovery guard and return deterministic
    `RECOVERY_BUSY` for nested entry.
  - [ ] Set `QUIESCING` immediately before each active owner's protected
    quiescence callback and resolve it to `QUIESCED` or `QUIESCE_FAILED` before
    returning.
  - [ ] Attempt the complete ordered quiescence pass before retirement.
  - [ ] Retire only owners that are already demonstrably `QUIESCED` or
    `RETIRE_FAILED`, using `RETIRING` as the protected transient state.
  - [ ] Attempt every eligible owner after individual failures.
  - [ ] Never retry or execute `QUIESCE_FAILED`; retain it for diagnostics and
    report process restart as required.
  - [ ] Retry `RETIRE_FAILED` without repeating successful quiescence.
  - [ ] Never repeat successful retirement.
  - [ ] Report every failure with owner identity, generation, operation, and
    traceback.
  - [ ] Detect and report future-generation records without allowing their
    labels to determine successor generation.
  - [ ] Pre-discard only successfully retired future-generation records before
    advancement.
  - [ ] Clear the recovery guard through protected finalization on every
    outcome.
- [ ] Implement `discard_retired_owners()`.
  - [ ] Acquire the same guard and reject nested entry as `RECOVERY_BUSY`.
  - [ ] Select only remaining `RETIRED` records and never repeat quiescence or
    retirement.
  - [ ] Invoke discard under protection and remove a record only after success.
  - [ ] Retain discard-failed `RETIRED` records for an explicit later retry.
  - [ ] Clear the guard through protected finalization on every outcome.
- [ ] Require discard callbacks to compare exact captured-state identity so
  they cannot clear a replacement owner.
- [ ] Use explicit idempotent no-op quiescence and retirement callbacks for
  discard-only records.
- [ ] Add focused failure-injection tests around every inactive-gate
  transition and callback boundary.
  - [ ] Prove deterministic order and complete best-effort aggregation.
  - [ ] Prove the proposal's approved per-owner quiescence prerequisite.
  - [ ] Prove exactly-once successful retirement and discard-only retry.
  - [ ] Prove current/older and future-generation selection differences.
  - [ ] Prove `QUIESCE_FAILED` is diagnostic-only and restart-blocking.
  - [ ] Prove malformed callbacks are never invoked.
  - [ ] Prove both operations reject nested entry and never return with a
    transient record status or a stuck guard.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of every state transition and failure branch
  before marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every valid status has one deterministic executable, retryable,
  diagnostic-only, or removable outcome.
- [ ] One owner failure cannot prevent later eligible owners from quiescing or
  retiring.
- [ ] Future records are removed before advancement or block advancement.
- [ ] Focused lifecycle, reentrancy, corruption, and retry tests pass.

## Phase 4: Register tooltip owners and preserve render-hook adoption

Outcome: All tooltip generation-owned state is coordinator-tracked while the
existing render trampoline remains process-stable and adoptable.

- [ ] Integrate coordinator registration into tooltip runtime, service,
  registration, map-target, presenter, and related namespace state publication.
  - [ ] Register only after each captured process state is fully publishable.
  - [ ] Roll back publication and any successful registration when construction
    cannot complete.
  - [ ] Use discard-only records for state with no active behavior.
- [ ] Split tooltip presenter shutdown into the proposal's quiescence and
  fallible retirement responsibilities.
  - [ ] Quiescence synchronously detaches intent observation and presenter
    dispatch, clears selected transport/owner dispatch, and gates the renderer
    inactive without invoking consumer code.
  - [ ] Retirement performs remaining fallible cleanup without reactivating
    dispatch.
- [ ] Preserve the exact `tooltip_render_hook` trampoline record across reload.
  - [ ] Keep its installed function identity stable for foreign wrapper chains
    and exact-identity cleanup.
  - [ ] Let the successor hook manager adopt the preserved record and advance
    its internal generation before accepting new ownership.
  - [ ] Prevent double presentation during retirement or adoption.
- [ ] Extend tooltip runtime, service, registration, presenter, and render-hook
  tests for complete and partial publication topologies.
- [ ] Prove tooltip quiescence, retirement, and discard callbacks act only on
  their exact captured owner state and cannot mutate replacement state.
- [ ] Prove old tooltip APIs and handles remain stale after advancement and are
  never made current by coordinator recovery.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of tooltip owner coverage and exact trampoline
  identity before marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every tooltip generation-owned process state has a coordinator record or
  an explicit process-stable adoption contract.
- [ ] Presenter dispatch is inert before its fallible retirement begins.
- [ ] The successor adopts the exact existing render trampoline without double
  presentation.
- [ ] Focused tooltip and coordinator integration tests pass.

## Phase 5: Register context-menu and input owners

Outcome: Context-menu sessions, registration state, and destructive input-hook
ownership participate in generic coordinator recovery.

- [ ] Integrate coordinator registration into context-menu service and
  registration-manager publication.
  - [ ] Quiesce active sessions and externally callable registration callbacks
    before fallible cleanup.
  - [ ] Close sessions, release registrations, and discard slots by exact
    captured-state identity.
- [ ] Register `context_menu_input_hook` as generation-owned and destructively
  retired.
  - [ ] Quiesce priority consumers and externally callable dispatch first.
  - [ ] Use `shutdown()` during retirement to restore/remove owned native and
    screen hooks.
  - [ ] Do not make this hook adoptable.
- [ ] Cover failure before and after each context-menu publication/registration
  boundary and prove rollback or retained tracking.
- [ ] Prove context-menu quiescence, retirement, and discard callbacks act only
  on their exact captured owner state and cannot mutate replacement state.
- [ ] Extend context-menu service, registration, input-hook, command reload,
  and failure-lifecycle tests.
- [ ] Preserve the required ordering after UserPrompt cancellation and before
  later tooltip/input cleanup.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of context-menu owner coverage and destructive
  hook cleanup before marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every context-menu generation-owned owner is tracked and discarded by
  exact identity.
- [ ] Active sessions and dispatch are revoked before fallible retirement.
- [ ] The input hook is destructively removed/restored and never adopted.
- [ ] Focused context-menu lifecycle and recovery tests pass.

## Phase 6: Register UserPrompt and shared input/render ownership

Outcome: UserPrompt cancellation and shared-seam release occur first in the
deterministic recovery order without invoking consumer callbacks unsafely.

- [ ] Integrate coordinator registration into UserPrompt runtime and service
  publication.
  - [ ] Remove the state-change callback during quiescence.
  - [ ] Cancel active work with the Core-reload cause under the established
    callback-safety contract.
  - [ ] Release shared context-menu input and tooltip render ownership in the
    proposal's deterministic order.
  - [ ] Discard the exact captured service/runtime state only after retirement.
- [ ] Prove quiescence never invokes consumer code and revokes externally
  callable dispatch before fallible retirement.
- [ ] Add failure injection around state-change callback removal, active-work
  cancellation, shared-owner release, retirement, and discard.
- [ ] Prove UserPrompt quiescence, retirement, and discard callbacks act only on
  their exact captured owner state and cannot mutate replacement state.
- [ ] Extend UserPrompt runtime, service, input-consumer, lifecycle, and command
  reload tests.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of UserPrompt-first ordering and shared-owner
  release before marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Active UserPrompt work cannot dispatch after successful quiescence.
- [ ] UserPrompt releases shared ownership before context-menu, tooltip, and
  remaining input owners retire.
- [ ] UserPrompt partial publication is rolled back or retained under an
  explicit coordinator status.
- [ ] Focused UserPrompt lifecycle and coordinator integration tests pass.

## Phase 7: Add reconstruction-failure recovery

Outcome: A failed successor construction cannot leak untracked active behavior;
every published partial owner is cleaned, retryable, or diagnostic-only before
command-level recovery is removed.

- [ ] Add coordinator-driven reconstruction-failure cleanup while the existing
  command-level recovery remains available.
- [ ] On reconstruction failure, use coordinator operations to attempt
  quiescence and retirement of every successfully registered successor owner.
- [ ] Attempt every eligible callback despite individual failures.
- [ ] Attempt discard of every successfully retired successor record.
- [ ] Retain `RETIRE_FAILED` and discard-failed `RETIRED` records for explicit
  in-process retry.
- [ ] Retain `QUIESCE_FAILED` records for diagnostics, block reconstruction,
  and require process restart.
- [ ] Clear partial service and facade caches without discarding coordinator
  records that still own retained state.
- [ ] Publish the successor runtime disabled before returning the combined
  reconstruction and cleanup report.
- [ ] Add failure injection after every publication boundary across tooltip,
  context-menu, and UserPrompt construction.
- [ ] Prove each partial topology is cleaned immediately, retained for retry,
  or retained diagnostic-only pending restart.
- [ ] Prove a corrected retry of retirement/discard failure reaches a healthy
  later generation without loading failed-generation owner scripts.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of every injected partial topology before
  marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] No reconstruction failure leaves untracked process-owned state.
- [ ] Successfully retired records are discarded or retained as `RETIRED` when
  discard fails.
- [ ] Retryable and restart-required states remain distinguishable.
- [ ] Focused reconstruction-failure and corrected-retry tests pass.

## Phase 8: Replace command-specific recovery with generic orchestration

Outcome: `command.lua` owns only the proposal's transaction sequence and has no
knowledge of service-specific process state or owner shapes.

- [ ] Refactor `src/scripts_modinstalled/dwarfuicore/command.lua` to:
  - [ ] load only the module registry, service runtime, and coordinator needed
    for transaction orchestration;
  - [ ] place the runtime in retiring state before cross-generation recovery;
  - [ ] invoke pre-advancement recovery and consume its aggregate result;
  - [ ] disable without advancing when any future-generation record remains;
  - [ ] otherwise publish exactly one initializing successor generation;
  - [ ] invoke post-advancement discard for remaining `RETIRED` records;
  - [ ] publish a disabled successor and stop before reconstruction after any
    current/older recovery failure, discard failure, malformed record, or
    restart blocker;
  - [ ] clear/reconstruct module environments only after safe cleanup;
  - [ ] validate registry contracts before publishing healthy state; and
  - [ ] aggregate and report lifecycle, reconstruction, and cleanup failures.
- [ ] Remove every tooltip, context-menu, UserPrompt, presenter,
  registration-manager, and hook-manager process slot or owner-shape reference
  from `command.lua`.
- [ ] Remove command-local generation comparisons for service owners; keep
  generation comparison inside the coordinator and service-runtime contracts.
- [ ] Keep `service_provider_runtime` outside coordinator records and use it
  only as the transaction authority for retiring, advancement, initialization,
  health publication, and disabled-state publication.
- [ ] Preserve old public object failure behavior.
  - [ ] Advanced-generation APIs return `STALE_API` and handles return
    `STALE_HANDLE`.
  - [ ] Same-generation APIs and handles return `SERVICE_UNHEALTHY` when a
    future-record failure disables the runtime without advancement.
- [ ] Extend command and service-runtime tests for all successful, disabled,
  malformed, retryable, restart-required, current/older, and future-record
  branches.
- [ ] Prove through production call-site inspection and focused tests that
  coordinator recovery operations are invoked only by the explicit reload
  transaction after the service runtime has entered retiring state.
- [ ] Prove ordinary module loading, service acquisition, API invocation, and
  handle validation never invoke coordinator recovery.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review proving `command.lua` contains orchestration
  only before marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] `command.lua` names no service-specific process slot, owner shape, or
  owner-generation comparison.
- [ ] Successful reload advances exactly once and reconstructs only after all
  required cleanup gates pass.
- [ ] Current/older failure advances to disabled; future failure disables
  without advancement.
- [ ] Coordinator recovery has no production entry path outside explicit reload
  after retiring state begins.
- [ ] Command and service-runtime focused suites pass.

## Phase 9: Registry, syntax, and package integration

Outcome: Every private coordinator artifact and test is included in dependency
order and distributed without expanding the public service surface.

- [ ] Reconfirm the previously established `reload_contracts` and
  `reload_coordinator` registry order after all lifecycle-registering consumers
  have been integrated.
- [ ] Update `tests/run.lua` for every new focused suite.
- [ ] Update `tests/package_contract.spec.lua` and
  `tools/VerifyPackage.ps1` so expanded and ZIP packages require the new private
  modules.
- [ ] Prove `dwarfuicore/services` exports no coordinator, lifecycle enum,
  diagnostic, quiescence, retirement, discard, or recovery API.
- [ ] Run focused registry, package-contract, public-export, and naming checks.
- [ ] Run `tools/Check-LuaSyntax.ps1` across production and test/support Lua.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of registry order, suite registration, package
  manifests, and public exports before marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] New modules load before all lifecycle-registering consumers.
- [ ] Syntax and package contracts include every new file exactly.
- [ ] The public provider surface is unchanged.
- [ ] Focused registry, package, export, naming, and syntax checks pass.

## Phase 10: Full source and package qualification

Outcome: Repository-local automated evidence proves the complete proposal
contract before live activation.

- [ ] Run all focused coordinator, command, service-runtime, tooltip,
  render-hook, context-menu, UserPrompt, API, and failure-recovery suites.
- [ ] Run the complete unit suite with `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` and record the
  exact terminal counts.
- [ ] Run Lua syntax validation and record the exact production and test/support
  file counts.
- [ ] Run repository build/package preparation through the existing project
  tools without installing the package.
- [ ] Run expanded package verification and ZIP verification separately and
  record their exact outcomes.
- [ ] Run `git diff --check` and inspect `git status --short` without altering
  unrelated worktree or index state.
- [ ] Perform the required implementation follow-up review against every task
  and acceptance criterion in this plan.
- [ ] Perform an independent completion audit before checking this phase or any
  final acceptance item.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Focused suites, full units, syntax, expanded package, ZIP package, and
  whitespace checks all pass with recorded evidence.
- [ ] No required result is inferred from a different evidence class.
- [ ] The completion audit reports no unresolved implementation or evidence
  discrepancy.

## Phase 11: Operator restart and terminal live source-reload acceptance

Outcome: Coordinator-bearing code is activated only in a fresh process, then a
terminal live source reload proves successor reconstruction without modifying
installed package files.

- [ ] Document the first-activation restart prerequisite and the unsupported
  pre-coordinator source-reload path.
- [ ] Wait for explicit operator confirmation that Dwarf Fortress was restarted
  before loading coordinator-bearing code.
- [ ] Do not restart Dwarf Fortress automatically or discard unsaved game
  state.
- [ ] Run the established live source-reload route against the current source
  registry without installing or modifying package files.
- [ ] Capture terminal live evidence for:
  - [ ] coordinator-aware owner registration from first publication;
  - [ ] successful explicit Core reload;
  - [ ] normal one-step generation advancement;
  - [ ] exact tooltip render-trampoline adoption without double presentation;
  - [ ] stale API/handle rejection; and
  - [ ] terminal cleanup confirmation.
- [ ] If the live route does not produce terminal status and cleanup evidence,
  record it as inconclusive or failed rather than complete.
- [ ] Perform a final follow-up review of live evidence and cleanup before
  marking this phase complete.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] The operator-controlled restart occurred before first activation.
- [ ] The live source reload completed terminally without package installation
  or installed-file modification.
- [ ] Required runtime behavior and cleanup were directly evidenced.

## Execution evidence ledger

- [ ] Record the approved quiescence invariant and its proposal amendment.
- [ ] Record focused characterization commands and terminal results.
- [ ] Record focused coordinator/state-machine commands and terminal results.
- [ ] Record focused tooltip, context-menu, UserPrompt, command, API, and
  reconstruction-recovery commands and terminal results.
- [ ] Record the full unit command and exact success/failure/error/pending
  counts.
- [ ] Record syntax command and exact production/test-support file counts.
- [ ] Record expanded and ZIP package verification commands and results
  separately.
- [ ] Record `git diff --check` and final scoped worktree status.
- [ ] Record operator restart confirmation, live command, terminal result, and
  cleanup confirmation.

## Final acceptance

- [ ] The approved per-owner quiescence invariant is implemented exactly.
- [ ] Every proposal requirement is implemented or explicitly deferred with an
  approved rationale.
- [ ] Every generation-owned tooltip, context-menu, and UserPrompt state has a
  coordinator record or explicit process-stable adoption contract.
- [ ] The complete quiescence pass and per-owner retirement eligibility follow
  the resolved proposal invariant.
- [ ] Successful retirement is exactly once; failed retirement and discard are
  retryable under their defined states; `QUIESCE_FAILED` remains diagnostic-only
  and requires restart.
- [ ] Future records are reported and removed before advancement or disable the
  current runtime without advancement.
- [ ] Malformed records are never invoked and require restart.
- [ ] Existing APIs and handles return stale errors after advancement and
  `SERVICE_UNHEALTHY` for a same-generation disabled runtime.
- [ ] Failed reconstruction cleans or retains every published partial owner
  under an explicit lifecycle state.
- [ ] Tooltip render-trampoline identity survives and is adopted exactly once.
- [ ] `command.lua` is generic transaction orchestration with no service slot,
  owner-shape, or owner-generation knowledge.
- [ ] New modules obey project module, `reqscript()`, LuaDoc, immutable-enum,
  registry-order, test-registration, and package rules.
- [ ] Focused, full-unit, syntax, expanded-package, ZIP-package, and terminal
  live evidence all satisfy their distinct claims.
- [ ] The first live activation follows an operator-controlled process restart;
  no in-process pre-coordinator migration is attempted.
- [ ] A follow-up implementation review and independent completion audit find
  no unresolved requirement or evidence gap.
